  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition
    
    The Hisper∴vi is a Nightshelf trade-festival held in the upper caverns of Nightfall Shelf, the labyrinthine colony carved into the Berg's shadowed interior. It is triggered — not scheduled — when the colony's trade-ledgers record surplus for the first time in three consecutive seasons. Created during the Frozen Peace, the reconstruction era following the Orca Incursion, the Hisper∴vi marks the moment when the Flipper Accord ceased to function as emergency rationing and began to resemble commerce again.
    
    The canonical summary states it commemorates not prosperity but *the realization that the colony might not starve.* The distinction matters.
    
    ## Observance
    
    The festival lasts three days. Merchants display fire-cores on ash-gray cloth while Courier Corps members descend from Aurora Stack bearing crystal bundles whose refracted light off the bioluminescent tunnel-markers constitutes the closest thing the event has to decoration. Shadow-Broker Nyla — leader of the Midnight Claws and the Nightshelf's primary trade coordinator until her recent confession before the Aurora council — called prices in the coded proverbs characteristic of Nightshelf speech. No formal ceremony structures the proceedings.
    
    The Hisper∴vi intersects with several Nightshelf governance-laws it is recorded as commemorating: the collective silence mandated by Foedus∴silenti, the architectural grief of Memoria∴moria, and the witness-obligation of Ritus∴um among them. Fermented kelp-wine circulates freely. Oral accounts of past disasters — particularly the flooding of the colony's eastern shaft, which killed seventeen miners — are recounted without interruption.
    
    The figure seventeen appears in every version I have encountered and I note it without further comment.
    
    ## The Crypt-seal Incident
    
    During one recorded Hisper∴vi, an Aurora courier delivered a volatile crystal shard — Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~, originating from the deep tunnels of Sleek☽ Caemomot — to a merchant's stall on the second day. The shard radiated abnormal warmth, drew traders physically closer, and accelerated fire-core sales at adjacent stations. The Icebound Exchange manifest noted only that the object required *proper containment* — a phrase that, in my experience with Exchange documentation, consistently understates the severity of the hazard by a considerable margin.
